PressboardKit Licence Agreement
===============================

DRAFT — NOT YET REVIEWED BY A LAWYER. The commercial terms below are
decided; the drafting of them is not. Nothing here has been read by
counsel, and two passages in square brackets flag the questions a lawyer
should answer first. Until that review happens, treat this as a statement
of intent rather than as a document to rely on.

Version 0.2 (draft), 22 September 2026.
Plain-language companion, kept in step with this file:
https://pressboardkit.com/legal/licence/


0. Licensor
-----------

    AFTG s.r.o.
    Komenského 1081/91, Bolevec, 323 00 Plzeň, Czech Republic
    IČO (company number): 03267725
    Registered in the Commercial Register kept by the Regional Court in
    Plzeň, file C 29968.

referred to below as "we" or "us". "PressboardKit" and the PressboardKit
mark are ours.


1. What this covers
-------------------

"the Software" means the PressboardKit engine — every module of the Swift
package, any binary framework we distribute in its place, the bundled
data described in section 7, and the documentation shipped with them.

"you" means the person or organisation to whom we issued a licence.

"your Application" means an iOS application, and its keyboard extension,
that you develop and publish under your own name.

This licence covers the Software. Use of our website is covered by the
site terms, not by this file.


2. Free, and licensed rather than open
--------------------------------------

The Software is free to use. There is no charge for a licence.

It is licensed to you, not sold, and it is not open source. You register
your Application, we issue a licence against it, and the Software
validates that licence — which is why registration exists and why section
8 matters: a licence is a relationship with terms, and this file is them.

3. What you may do
------------------

Subject to section 4, you may:

  (a) embed the Software — compiled into your Application's binary and
      its keyboard extension — in Applications you develop and publish
      under your own name;

  (b) distribute those Applications to end users through the App Store or
      any other channel Apple permits, with no per-install fee and no
      limit on the number of end users;

  (c) configure and extend the Software through its public API: styling,
      layouts, suggestion providers, feedback, and every switch on
      KeyboardBehavior;

  (d) use the Software in internal builds, test harnesses and demo builds
      of those same Applications.

Source access is not included. The licence covers the Software in the
form we distribute it; it does not give you a copy of the source, and
there is therefore nothing to modify and ship.


4. What you may not do
----------------------

You may not:

  (a) redistribute the Software itself — as source, as a package, as a
      binary framework, or as part of a template, starter kit or code
      generator — to anyone other than your own developers;

  (b) resell the Software, or offer it in whole or in substantial part as
      a keyboard SDK, library or service that competes with
      PressboardKit;

  (c) sublicense, assign or transfer this licence, except as set out in
      section 9;

  (d) remove, disable or circumvent any licence check, including the
      LicenseGate seam, or remove or obscure any notice identifying the
      Software's authorship;

  (e) decompile, disassemble or otherwise reverse engineer any binary
      form of the Software, except to the extent that this restriction is
      unenforceable under applicable law.

Building an Application that happens to be a keyboard is expressly fine.
Building a product whose value is that it gives other developers this
engine is not. The Software being free changes neither half of that.


5. Registration, licence keys and validation
--------------------------------------------

A licence is issued against an Application. You register with an email
address we can serve notice to, you name the Application — its product
name and its bundle identifier — and we issue a licence and a key. There
is no charge and no approval step.

The Software validates that licence. Concretely:

  (a) Your containing application exchanges the licence key for a signed
      **licence lease** naming the product name and bundle identifier the
      licence was issued against, and stores it where the keyboard
      extension can read it.

  (b) The keyboard extension verifies that signature **offline**, against
      a key compiled into the Software. **The keyboard extension does not
      open a network connection.** It has no code that could.

  (c) What leaves the containing application is the licence key and the
      bundle identifier. No text you or your users type, no keystrokes,
      no device identifier and no identifier of an individual
      installation are sent to us, at any point, by any part of the
      Software.

  (d) Validation fails **open**. No lease, an unreadable one, a signature
      that does not verify, a bundle identifier that does not match, a
      clock that has moved, or a service of ours that is down — every one
      of them leaves the free tier running. A keyboard somebody is typing
      on does not stop because of anything in this section.

Every deadline the Software enforces arrives inside the lease rather than
compiled into it, so that a mistake of ours can be corrected without you
shipping a build.

Attempting to defeat this mechanism is a breach of section 4(d).


6. Ownership
------------

We own the Software and every intellectual property right in it. This
licence grants you the rights set out in section 3 and nothing else; no
right is granted by implication. You own your Application, including any
code you write against the Software's public API.


7. Bundled data and third-party components
------------------------------------------

The Software declares no third-party runtime dependencies and links only
Apple's own frameworks.

It does ship word-frequency lists used by the autocomplete module. All
39 derive from Hermit Dave's "FrequencyWords" (2018 edition), built from
the OpenSubtitles corpus. **Those lists are licensed to you under
Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA
4.0), not under this agreement.** Per-language provenance, the statement
of what we changed, and the licence terms are in

    Sources/PressboardKitAutocomplete/Resources/CREDITS.txt

Redistributing them inside your Application is permitted by that licence,
and so is taking them out of it and using them elsewhere on CC BY-SA
terms.

To be unambiguous, because CC BY-SA 4.0 section 2(a)(5)(B) forbids us
from imposing terms that would stop you exercising those rights:
**section 4 does not apply to these word lists.** Nothing in 4(a) or 4(e)
restricts your right to extract, copy, adapt or redistribute them under
CC BY-SA 4.0. Section 4 restricts the engine; the data travels under its
own licence and always did.

    [PLACEHOLDER FOR THE LAWYER: this carve-out is the standard way to
    ship CC BY-SA data inside closed software, but it has not been
    reviewed. Confirm it is sufficient, and that shipping ShareAlike data
    inside a binary framework does not reach the framework itself. If it
    is not sufficient, the alternative is replacing the lists with
    permissively licensed ones.]


8. Changes to these terms
-------------------------

We may change these terms, **including what a licence costs**. Where a
change affects a registered licensee we will say so at the registration
email address we hold, which is part of why registration exists.

Where a change would require you to start paying for something that was
free, we will write **at least three months** before it takes effect. You
are not obliged to accept it; section 8a says what happens if you do not.


8a. What a change would mean for an Application you have already published
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the part that reaches past you, so it is stated plainly rather
than buried: **an Application you have already published goes on
working.** It keeps typing, it keeps installing, and we will not ask you
to withdraw it.

If a change introduces a charge and you do not take a paid licence, what
continues to work is the free tier — in new builds and in published
Applications alike — and any feature outside it stops. Nothing stops
mid-sentence on someone's phone: the licence lease the Software checks is
granted for a period rather than per launch, it fails open when it cannot
be reached, and it closes only on an expiry it has actually seen.

    [PLACEHOLDER FOR THE LAWYER: this section is the one most likely to
    need reworking. It reaches into software already on end users'
    devices, and those end users are not parties to this agreement.
    Confirm it is enforceable against a Czech or EU licensee, and whether
    the three months should be longer.]


8b. If a licence ever carries a charge
--------------------------------------

Nothing in this section applies today. It is here so that the terms of a
paid licence are settled in advance rather than written at the moment we
would benefit from writing them.

A paid licence would be a **subscription**, billed monthly or annually as
you choose, unlocking every feature for every version released while it
is current.

If it lapsed, section 8a describes what you are left with. That would
apply **30 days after a payment is missed**, not immediately — a failed
card should not take features out of an app that people are using — and
not before we have actually reached you at the registration address.

If **we** discontinue PressboardKit, the last version licensed to you
becomes yours perpetually, on the terms in force when we discontinue it.
You should not lose what you have paid for because we stopped.

Support is best effort. There is no service level, no guaranteed response
time, and we would rather say so than publish a number we cannot hold to.
Email and the public issue tracker are the channels.


9. Transfer
-----------

This licence transfers with a sale. If you sell the Application it covers
— or your business — the licence goes with it, and the buyer takes it on
these terms. Tell us in writing within 30 days; our agreement is not
required.

What does not change is the scope. The licence covers the Application it
covered before the sale, not the buyer's other products. A buyer who
wants it to reach further takes out a licence of their own, at the terms
then current.


10. No warranty
---------------

THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS
OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF
MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT.

We do not warrant that the Software is free of defects, that it will meet
your requirements, or that its behaviour will match any particular
version of iOS. The Software reproduces the behaviour of Apple's keyboard
as measured against specific iOS releases; Apple may change that
behaviour at any time.


11. Limitation of liability
---------------------------

TO THE MAXIMUM EXTENT PERMITTED BY APPLICABLE LAW, IN NO EVENT SHALL WE
BE LIABLE FOR ANY INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, CONSEQUENTIAL OR
PUNITIVE DAMAGES, OR FOR ANY LOSS OF PROFITS, REVENUE, DATA OR GOODWILL,
ARISING OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THIS LICENCE.

To the same extent, we exclude liability altogether rather than cap it.
There is no figure here because there is no sum we accept: you take the
Software as it is and carry the risk of using it.

That exclusion is not unlimited, and cannot be. Czech law (§ 2898 of the
Civil Code) disregards any agreement made in advance that excludes or
limits the duty to compensate harm caused **intentionally or by gross
negligence**, or harm to a person's **natural rights**. Those remain,
whatever this section says, and no drafting removes them.


12. Termination
---------------

This section is about breach. A change to these terms is a different
event and is dealt with in sections 8 and 8a.

This licence ends if you breach it and do not remedy the breach within
30 days of us telling you about it. On termination you must stop
embedding the Software in new builds. Applications already published
under it are not affected — we will not ask you to withdraw a shipped
app.


13. Governing law and jurisdiction
----------------------------------

This licence is governed by the law of the Czech Republic, excluding its
conflict-of-law rules and excluding the United Nations Convention on
Contracts for the International Sale of Goods.

The courts of the Czech Republic have exclusive jurisdiction over any
dispute arising out of it, the locally competent court being the one for
our registered seat.

    [NOTE FOR THE LAWYER: a consumer or a business in another EU member
    state may be entitled to its own forum regardless of this clause.
    Confirm how far exclusive jurisdiction actually holds.]


